## Authorization — seeding the data plane (the standard path)

This is the section people come for. To make CRUD work for a principal on an
entity, insert `policy_rules` rows with the exact tokens from *Surface 1*.
The straightforward path is **`udb authz seed`** (Option D below): offline,
validated action tokens, idempotent + atomic.

### Option A — from app code, effective immediately

Call `AuthzService.PutAuthzPolicy` (full rule incl. role/purpose/required_scopes)
or `CreatePolicyRule` (minimal). One Allow per (subject-or-role, tenant, object,
action) — or collapse with `action="*"` / `object="*"`.

```txt
# grant a role read+write on one entity for a tenant, then bind principals to it
PutAuthzPolicy { subject: "role:app_rw", tenant_id: "<T-UUID>", object: "shop.v1.Customer",
                 action: "*", effect: POLICY_EFFECT_ALLOW }
AssignRole   { principal: "<user-or-service user_id>", role: "app_rw", tenant_id: "<T-UUID>" }
```
Bind BOTH your human users and your service accounts to `app_rw` and you never
touch policies again as identities come and go.

### Option B — from the CLI, effective immediately

```bash
udb auth policy put --subject role:app_rw --action '*' \
    --resource shop.v1.Customer --effect allow --tenant <T-UUID> --project <proj>
udb auth role bind --principal <user_id> --role app_rw --tenant <T-UUID>
```

### Option C — offline, version-controlled seed → psql

Author a JSON array (the `AuthzPolicy` offline shape) and apply it:
```json
[{"effect":"allow","subject":"role:app_rw","tenant":"<T-UUID>","resource":"shop.v1.Customer","action":"Select"},
 {"effect":"allow","subject":"role:app_rw","tenant":"<T-UUID>","resource":"shop.v1.Customer","action":"Upsert"},
 {"effect":"allow","subject":"role:app_rw","tenant":"<T-UUID>","resource":"shop.v1.Customer","action":"Delete"},
 {"effect":"allow","subject":"role:app_rw","tenant":"<T-UUID>","resource":"shop.v1.Customer","action":"Update"}]
```
```bash
UDB_ABAC_POLICY_FILE=authz-policies.json udb policy-seed | psql "$UDB_PG_DSN"
```
`udb policy-seed` prints a `BEGIN; DELETE …; INSERT …; COMMIT;` transaction for
`udb_authz.policy_rules` — atomic by construction (which matters: recall the
default-allow flip). Commit this file to your repo as the project's policy
scaffold.

### Option D — `udb authz seed`: offline, validated, idempotent (the straightforward path)

```bash
UDB_PG_DSN="$DSN" udb authz seed --tenant <T-UUID> --role app_rw
# → Allow rows for Select/Upsert/Delete/Update/BulkCas on `*`, role app_rw
UDB_PG_DSN="$DSN" udb authz seed --tenant <T-UUID> --role app_rw \
    --entity shop.v1.Customer --emit authz-policies.json
# → narrowed to one entity; --emit keeps a version-controlled copy
```
Postgres-direct (no running broker), idempotent + atomic, and `--action` is
validated against the real RPC method names — a `data.*` alias is rejected up
front. Repeat `--entity` to narrow the wildcard; then bind principals exactly
as in the other options (`udb auth role bind --principal <id> --role app_rw
--tenant <T-UUID>`).

### Checklist that prevents 90% of denies
- `action` ∈ `{Select, Upsert, Delete, Update, BulkCas, BatchSelect,
  BatchUpsert, *}` — never `data.select`/`read`/`write`.
- `object` = the message_type FQN (or `*` / glob).
- `tenant_id` = the caller's tenant **UUID** (or empty/`*`).
- Prefer a **role** subject + role bindings over per-identity rows.
- Leave `required_scopes` and `purpose` empty for a first bootstrap (they refine
  an Allow — a populated `required_scopes` the caller lacks turns the Allow off).
- Seed **all** rows atomically (default-allow flips off at row #1).
- The caller sends a non-empty `x-purpose` and the tenant UUID.

### Casbin cookbook — common patterns as worked rows

The Casbin model is `r = sub, dom, obj, act`; `p = sub, dom, obj, act, eft`;
`g = _, _` (role grouping). The matcher: subject via `g()` or `p.sub == "*"`;
`dom` exact or `*`/empty; `obj` via `keyMatch2`; `act` exact / `keyMatch2` / `*`.
Deny beats allow (deny-override). `policy_rules` columns map:
`policy.tenant ← tenant_id`, `policy.resource ← object`, and `role`/`purpose`/
`required_scopes`/`priority` live in `attributes_json`. Worked patterns
(showing `subject | tenant_id | object | action | effect`):

- **Read-only role on everything, one tenant:**
  `role:reader | <T-UUID> | * | Select | ALLOW`
  Bind users: `AssignRole{ principal, role:"reader", tenant_id:<T> }`.
- **Read+write role on everything:** two rows `role:app_rw | <T> | * | Select |
  ALLOW` and `… | Upsert | ALLOW` (plus `Delete`/`Update`), or **one** row with
  `action: *`.
- **Per-entity least privilege:** `role:orders_writer | <T> | shop.v1.Order |
  Upsert | ALLOW`. Narrow `object` to the FQN.
- **Package glob:** `role:shop_admin | <T> | shop.v1.* | * | ALLOW` (keyMatch2
  on `object`).
- **A specific service account, no role:** `svc-user-uuid | <T> | * | * | ALLOW`
  — or match by identity string with `service:billing.api`.
- **Cross-tenant admin (careful):** leave `tenant_id` empty/`*`:
  `role:platform_admin |  | * | * | ALLOW`.
- **Explicit deny (wins over any allow):** `role:contractor | <T> |
  hr.v1.Salary | * | DENY` — a targeted carve-out on top of a broad allow.
- **Scope-refined allow:** put `required_scopes:["udb:write"]` in the row's
  attributes so the Allow only matches callers holding that scope (leave empty
  for a first bootstrap — a scope the caller lacks silently turns the allow off).

Verify a decision without a live call: `udb authz simulate` (read-only "what
would this bundle change"), or `udb doctor --consumer --key <k> --entity <fqn>`
(runs the real chain and prints the reason). Roles/tuples: `AssignRole` writes a
`policy_tuples` grouping row; ReBAC uses `tuple_kind='relationship'`.

