## CRUD (the data plane) + semantics that matter

All data RPCs take `message_type` = the proto's fully-qualified name.

- **Select**: `{ message_type, filter, limit, order_by }` → `RecordSet`. Page
  sizes are server-capped (default max 500 rows/page) — paginate. Page tokens are
  query-bound: page the SAME query.
- **Insert / Upsert**: `{ message_type, record/record_json, conflict_fields,
  return_record, idempotency_key }`. **There is no separate `Insert` RPC.** See
  *Inserting a new row* below. **Always set `conflict_fields`** for merge
  semantics, and set `idempotency_key` on writes you may retry.
  Retry contract: SDKs auto-retry *read-only* RPCs on transient errors; a
  **replay-safe mutation (Upsert/Delete/Update) is retried ONLY with a non-empty
  `idempotency_key`** — keyless mutations fail closed rather than double-apply. A
  keyed mutation is **durably deduplicated broker-side**: a replay returns the
  stored first-writer response with `was_duplicate=true` (check via the SDK
  helper — `WasDuplicate`/`wasDuplicate`/`was_duplicate`/`wasReplay`/
  `MutationOutcome`).
- **Update**: partial `SET`/atomic increments on matched rows — no full-record
  resend. Respects proto3 presence.
- **Delete**: `{ message_type, filter }` (tombstones on `soft_delete` tables).
- **BulkCas**: bounded tenant-scoped batch of conditional single-row updates in
  one tx; per-item CAS miss → `conflicted`, safe to retry with the same key.
- **Typed stores** (backend-permitting): cache get/set/delete/scan, document
  get/find/upsert/delete, graph query/mutate, time-series write/query,
  analytical query — advertised in `GetCapabilities.supported_rpcs`.

### Query & filter language

Filters are JSON objects keyed by proto **field names** (or column names).
`{"status": "active"}` is shorthand for equality. Operators are MongoDB-style:

| Form | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `{"col": v}` or `{"col": {"$eq": v}}` / `{"=": v}` | equals |
| `{"col": {"$in": [a,b]}}` | in a set |
| `{"col": {"$gt": v}}` / `$gte` / `$lt` / `$lte` | comparisons |
| `{"col": {"$ne": v}}` | not equal |
| `{"col": {"$is_null": true}}` | IS NULL / IS NOT NULL |
| `{"$and": [ …clauses… ]}` (or `"and"`) | all must hold |
| `{"$or": [ …clauses… ]}` (or `"or"`) | any must hold |

Rules that bite:
- **`{"col": null}` is NOT "IS NULL".** A bare `null` compares `col = NULL`, which
  is always UNKNOWN → matches **zero rows** (the broker rejects it on writes and
  ignores it on reads). Use `{"col": {"$is_null": true}}`. Many SDKs serialize a
  language `nil`/`None` field to JSON `null` — guard against accidentally sending
  it.
- Tenant-scoped reads/deletes **must pin `tenant_id`** (the UUID) somewhere in the
  filter, or `INVALID_ARGUMENT: tenant isolation requires filter on tenant_id`.
- A conditional `Update`/`Delete` that must target exactly one row pins the full
  primary key by equality; a non-equality operator on a PK column is rejected
  (`FAILED_PRECONDITION`) so a CAS can't accidentally hit many rows.
- `order_by` takes `field asc|desc`; pagination is server-capped and page tokens
  are query-bound (page the SAME filter/sort/fields).

Common shapes:
```txt
Select {message_type:"shop.v1.Order",
        filter:{"$and":[{"tenant_id":"<UUID>"},{"status":{"$in":["open","paid"]}}]},
        order_by:"created_at desc", limit:50}
Update {message_type:"shop.v1.Order", filter:{"tenant_id":"<UUID>","order_id":"o1"},
        set:{"status":"shipped"}, increment:{"version":1}, expected_revision:"<etag>"}
Delete {message_type:"shop.v1.Order", filter:{"tenant_id":"<UUID>","order_id":"o1"}}
```

### Inserting a new row (there is no `Insert` RPC)

Call `Upsert` with an **empty `conflict_fields`**. Two regimes:
- **Database-assigned PK** (`BIGSERIAL`/`SERIAL`/`gen_random_uuid()` default) and
  you omit the id → this **always appends a new row** (nothing to conflict on).
  Set `return_record: true` to get the assigned id back. This is the append-only
  insert.
- **Client-supplied PK** → `ON CONFLICT (pk) DO UPDATE` fires if the PK exists →
  this **merges (upsert)**, not a strict "fail if exists" create.
For an idempotent insert, pass `idempotency_key` (a bare retried insert on a
serial PK would otherwise create a *second* row). If your entity has a serial PK
plus two or more `*_id` columns and you get an
`identity field is ambiguous` error, set `return_record: true` (the response then
names the row by its assigned id) or name the identity via `conflict_fields`.

Records are JSON-shaped objects keyed by proto field names. Decode helpers turn
rows into language objects. **Sensitive fields never come back** — columns
annotated storage-only/redacted (password hashes, key material) are blanked
server-side.

**Read-your-writes, receipts, fences, consistency modes.** A mutation response
carries a **write receipt** (source LSN, outbox sequence, projection task ids,
manifest checksum). A follow-up read can carry a **read fence** built from it so
the broker waits until the write is visible, and/or an explicit **consistency
mode** (`strong`/`read_your_writes`/`bounded_staleness`/`replica_bounded`/
`eventual`/`projection_ok`/`cache_ok`). Every SDK packages this
(`metadata.afterWrite(receipt)` / `withConsistency(...)`). Prefer a fence over a
proof `Get` after every write.

**Typed errors.** Failed RPCs carry a decodable `udb.entity.v1.ErrorDetail` in
the `udb-error-detail-bin` trailer (kind, retryable, retry_after_ms,
field_violations). Every SDK decodes it — read `retryable`/`retry_after_ms`
instead of pattern-matching message strings.

