## New-project bootstrap — the runbook (do these in order)

The exact path from "nothing" to a first authenticated, authorized CRUD. Each
step removes one common failure.

**1. Define** the entity proto(s) with `table`/`column` annotations + a tenant
column; `import "udb/core/common/v1/db.proto"`. (Serving needs no `udb proto
export`/`buf` — the broker embeds the annotation contract; export/gen only for
vendored protos or typed rows.)

**2. Mint the first admin OFFLINE** (the DB has no principal yet). Capture the
printed **canonical tenant UUID**:
```bash
UDB_PG_DSN="postgres://udb:udb@host:5432/udb?sslmode=disable" \
UDB_PASSWORD_HASH_SECRET="<hmac-secret>" \
udb auth bootstrap user --username admin --email admin@x.com \
    --password '<strong-pass>' --tenant acme --project default
# → prints tenant_id, e.g. 00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000000d0001  (the UUID, NOT "acme")
```

**3. SEED authorization, or every data RPC is `PERMISSION_DENIED`** (default-deny;
the org-owner wildcard covers the admin only). Straightforward path — offline,
validated action tokens, idempotent + atomic:
```bash
UDB_PG_DSN="$DSN" udb authz seed --tenant <TENANT_UUID> --role app_rw
# → Allow Select/Upsert/Delete/Update/BulkCas on * for role app_rw
# then bind principals to the role (runtime): udb auth role bind --principal <id> --role app_rw --tenant <UUID>
```
Version-controlled alternative (same `policy_rules` table): author the offline
JSON using the **real action tokens** and the **tenant UUID**, seed atomically:
```json
[{"effect":"allow","subject":"role:app_rw","tenant":"<TENANT_UUID>","resource":"*","action":"Select"},
 {"effect":"allow","subject":"role:app_rw","tenant":"<TENANT_UUID>","resource":"*","action":"Upsert"},
 {"effect":"allow","subject":"role:app_rw","tenant":"<TENANT_UUID>","resource":"*","action":"Delete"},
 {"effect":"allow","subject":"role:app_rw","tenant":"<TENANT_UUID>","resource":"*","action":"Update"}]
```
```bash
UDB_ABAC_POLICY_FILE=authz-policies.json udb policy-seed | psql "$UDB_PG_DSN"
```
(`udb authz seed --emit authz-policies.json` produces exactly this file.)
Or from app code: `AuthzService.PutAuthzPolicy` + `AssignRole`. Dev shortcut:
`UDB_ABAC_DEFAULT_ALLOW=true` (only until row #1 exists).

**3b. (If you have a backend SERVICE)** provision its identity on `:50061`:
```txt
CreateUser{ account_kind: ACCOUNT_KIND_SERVICE_ACCOUNT, tenant_id:<UUID>, username:"billing-svc" }
CreateServiceAccountGrant{ user_id:<svc>, service_identity:"billing.api", tenant_id:<UUID>,
                           approved_scopes:["udb:read","udb:write"], reason:"billing writes" }
CreateApiKey{ owner_id:<svc>, owner_type: SERVICE_ACCOUNT, scopes:["udb:read","udb:write"] } → save plain_key
AssignRole{ principal:<svc>, role:"app_rw", tenant_id:<UUID> }   # so the SAME Casbin policy covers it
```

**4. Set the enterprise env the broker needs to boot + accept login**, then serve:
```bash
UDB_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY=<pem|path>  UDB_JWT_PUBLIC_KEY=<pem|path>   # RS256
UDB_SESSION_ENABLED=true  UDB_SESSION_HASH_SECRET=<hmac>        # else Authenticate → FAILED_PRECONDITION
UDB_PASSWORD_HASH_SECRET=<hmac>                                # SAME as step 2
UDB_AUTH_GRPC_ADDR=0.0.0.0:50061                               # expose the auth plane (default loopback)
udb serve proto "" 0.0.0.0:50051     # wait for "… is ready: data=… auth=…"
```
Run **`udb doctor --enterprise`** and **`udb requirements`** FIRST — they list
every unmet prerequisite/backend at once, not one restart at a time.

**5. Login → adopt the canonical tenant UUID → CRUD.** Connect with BOTH `target`
(`:50051`) and `authTarget` (`:50061`). `login()` → verify bearer →
`setTenant(principal.tenant_id)` (the UUID) → `Select`/`Upsert`/`Delete` by
`message_type`, **with `tenant_id` (UUID) in every filter** and a **non-empty
`purpose`**. Use `conflict_fields`/`idempotency_key` on writes.

> The live, end-to-end-verified version of this runbook (RS256 login, seeded
> policy, tenant-scoped CRUD of a generated `Invoice`, cross-tenant isolation
> check) is `examples/ts_enterprise/` — read its `README.md`,
> `scripts/bootstrap.sh` (offline admin mint + UUID parse + atomic policy seed +
> env emission), and `src/main.ts`. The Go/Python counterparts are
> `examples/go_enterprise/` and `examples/python_enterprise/`. Broker-side
> hardening (TLS/mTLS, pooler-safe DSN) is in `docs/enterprise-deployment.md`.

**Per-language ergonomics for steps 4–5:**
- **Go:** `udbclient.ConnectEnterprise(ctx, EnterpriseConfig{Target, AuthTarget,
  Username, Password, TenantCode, …})` → `EnterpriseSession` that logged in,
  adopted the UUID, and refreshes the bearer; use `session.DataContext(ctx)` /
  `session.NativeContext(ctx)` and `session.CanonicalTenantID` in filters;
  `session.ValidateTenant(id)` fails fast on a code-vs-UUID mismatch. (For a pure
  service account use the static `NewUdb` + `Credentials.APIKey` path — no
  refresh token exists for service accounts.)
- **TS:** `UdbProject.connectEnterprise(config)` or the manual
  `login → authenticateBearer → setTenant(principal.tenant_id)` steps.
- All SDKs auto-attach `x-request-id`/`x-correlation-id` so native RPCs don't
  fail `request context required`.

